`extern` declares a global; it does not define one.

WHAT IT IS. The keyword was skipped and the declaration fell through to the
ordinary global path, which EMITS STORAGE:

    extern char** environ;        ->        :GLOBAL_environ

So every translation unit that declared an extern global got its own
zero-initialised copy of it, and hex2 has no duplicate-label diagnostic to
notice. The unit that READS the variable and the unit that INITIALISES it were
two different objects.

WHAT IT COST. micro-c-libc's headers declare `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr`,
`errno` and `environ` this way, and libtcc.c includes them. tcc has therefore
been writing every diagnostic through a null `stderr` of its own rather than
M2libc's. That explains something this series misdiagnosed earlier: a
"compilation failed" message that never appeared, put down at the time to
stdio buffering.

It also silently disabled two rounds of instrumentation. micro-c-libc had a
getenv() sitting over `extern char** environ`, and NOTHING anywhere defines
`environ`. The reference resolved only because micro-c invented a null global
for it, so getenv returned NULL for every lookup, and two separate bisections
were run with environment switches -- PREDEF_LINES, KEYWORD_LIMIT -- that did
nothing at all. Both sets of readings were void, and neither announced itself.

WHY NO CASE CAUGHT IT. All 65 cases in tools/cases/ are a single file. A
LINKAGE bug is structurally invisible to a one-unit test -- there is no second
unit for the symbol to disagree with. This is the same shape as the
array-of-pointers gap, where every probe used `long*` so element width and
pointed-at width were both 8 and no case could tell them apart. A suite can
only find bugs in the shapes it is able to express.

WHAT THE FIX EXPOSED, both correctly:

  * micro-c-libc's getenv referenced an `environ` that does not exist. With
    extern no longer inventing one, the link fails. Removed -- M2libc's
    stdlib.c has a real getenv, and having two was the problem.
  * `environ` itself still has to exist, because tcc references it at
    tccrun.c:213 on the `-run` path. It is now DEFINED once, explicitly null,
    with a note that -run would need _start to pass envp. This spike only ever
    uses -c, and a null there fails immediately and obviously rather than
    subtly.

MEASURED

    difftest      64 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    tcc           builds and links; stdout/stderr resolve to M2libc's real
                  globals for the first time
    the open two-slot write into table_ident is unaffected and still open

diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index 6e6b1cf..467146a 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -5822,6 +5822,23 @@ int global_array_initializer_list(struct type* type_size, int array_modifier)
 
 int global_static_array(struct type* type_size, char* name)
 {
+	/* AN `extern` DECLARATION EMITS NOTHING. The symbol is already registered
+	 * by the caller, so references resolve to `&GLOBAL_<name>` -- which the
+	 * unit that DEFINES it supplies. Emitting storage here is what produced
+	 * two objects with one name; see the comment in cc_types.c. */
+	if(extern_declaration)
+	{
+		extern_declaration = FALSE;
+		/* consume to the end of the declaration without emitting */
+		while(NULL != global_token)
+		{
+			if(global_token->s[0] == ';') break;
+			if(global_token->s[0] == ',') break;
+			require_extra_token();
+		}
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	global_variable_header(name);
 
 	if(global_token->s[0] == ';' || global_token->s[0] == ',')
